sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/levin-2023-glue-09> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "In planarian flatworms the number of heads a regenerating fragment builds is set by the state of a bioelectric circuit with a default of one; transiently modifying it re-sets it to 'two', and the resulting two-headed worms keep regenerating as two-headed through multiple rounds of cutting without further manipulation, unless the pattern is experimentally reset.";
    dc:type "experimental-result";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Bioelectrically induced two-headed planaria stay two-headed across later regenerations";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Levin M, “Bioelectric networks: the cognitive glue enabling evolutionary scaling from physiology to mind”, Animal cognition (2023), doi:10.1007/s10071-023-01780-3; section “Basal cognition without neurons: what dynamic bodies think about › Re-writing morphogenetic memories: ontogenetic and evolutionary timescales”. Scope: Planarian flatworms (Dugesia japonica), author's lab (Oviedo et al. 2010; Durant et al. 2019)"
}
